(function(){if(!window.__hermes_register_book)return;window.__hermes_register_book("anacreon-fragments",[{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"The Fragments of Anacreon"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<figure class=\"reader-figure\"><img class=\"reader-figure-img\" src=\"data:image/jpeg;base64,\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Anacreon depicted singing and playing his lyre — Roman copy, second century AD, Copenhagen<\/figcaption><\/figure>"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"BOOK I"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"To Artemis"},{"st":"1","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion on Poems<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A poem is known as &lsquo;common in form&rsquo; when it is made up of two &lsquo;systems&rsquo; or stanzas like the first poem of Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">To thee I kneel, [lit. &lsquo;clasp thy knees in supplication&rsquo;] thou shooter of deer, flaxen-haired child of Zeus, Artemis queen of wild beasts, who now doubtless lookest down rejoicing beside the eddies of Lethaeus upon a city of valiant hearts; for thou art shepherd to no savage flock of men. [now: dedicatory of a new temple or statue of Artemis? city: Ionian Magnesia; the poem is prob. complete; see however Kehrhahn Berm. 1914]<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">For although according to the edition now in use the strophe has eight lines and the poem consists of a single strophe, this strophe can also be separated into a three-group and a five-group, so that a Pherecratic ends both the three-line and the four-line systems. [does not necessarily imply that 1. 3 ended in the Aristarchean (?) edition in θηρίων, but prob. that there was no division mark put between ll. 3 and 4.]<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"To Dionysus"},{"st":"2","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Dio Chrysostom Declamations<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">It follows that we should not offer to the king prayers like those we offer others, nor yet call upon the Gods in the manner of Anacreon the poet of the Ionians:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">O Lord with whom playeth Love the subduer and the dark-eyed Nymphs and rosy Aphrodite as thou wanderest the tops of the lofty hills, to thee I kneel; do thou come unto me kind and lending ear unto a prayer that is acceptable, and give Cleobulus good counsel, O Dionysus, to receive my love.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-note\">[a complete letter (or serenade?) to Cleobulus]<\/span>"},{"st":"3","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">[Herodian] on Figures of Speech [repetition of a word in various cases]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">It occurs in Anacreon in three:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">I love Cleobulus, I dote on Cleobulus, I gaze at Cleobulus.<\/span>"},{"st":"4","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on the eyes of the beloved]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And what says Anacreon?<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">O lad that lookest in maiden wise, I seek thee and thou hearkenest not, little knowing that the reins of my soul are in thy hand.<\/span>"},{"st":"5","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Eustathius on the Odyssey [&lsquo;thrice blest&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">... and in Anacreon:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">but O thrice out-swept Smerdies,<br>that is &lsquo;often swept out&rsquo; [meaning doubtful, perh. &lsquo;well-groomed, foppish&rsquo;]<\/span>"},{"st":"6","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same on the Iliad<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">... Poseidon is the &lsquo;cause&rsquo; comprising the sea, being the cause of &lsquo;drinking&rsquo; (ποδις) owing to the rivers and other waters which spring forth after percolating from the sea, with which &lsquo;drinking&rsquo; is connected the rain, itself &lsquo;drinkable&rsquo; (πάτιμος); and that is why in Attic the month of the winter solstice is called Poseideon; compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Lo! the month of Poseidon is here; the clouds are heavy with water, and wild storms bring the sky-God down.<\/span>"},{"st":"7","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Iliad [&lsquo;immovable&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The mark is against αστεμφες, which means &lsquo;not to be moved&rsquo;; compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">for thou to me wast immovable.<\/span>"},{"st":"8","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Dionysius Periegetes<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">... Tartessus which Anacreon calls all-happy, for that Arganthonius reigned there.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Strabo Geography [on the wealth of the Iberians]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Well might one believe the inhabitants of these parts to have a name for happiness and longevity, particularly their rulers; and it was for this reason Anacreon said:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">I would not have Amalthea&rsquo;s horn, nor even a reign of a hundred years and fifty over all-happy Tartessus; [the possessor of A.&rsquo;s horn got all he wished]<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Herodotus adds the name of the king in question, Arganthonius.<\/span>"},{"st":"9","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on sweet smells]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And the wise Anacreon says:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Come haste thee to anoint with unguent a bosom as hollow as a cave; [the idea is &lsquo;as deep-breasted as a woman&rsquo;] exhorting him to anoint the bosom, in which lies the heart, clearly because the heart is soothed by sweet scents.<\/span>"},{"st":"10","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Magnum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ΝΕΝΩΤΑΙ [for νενοηται] &lsquo;he is minded... and the participle in Anacreon:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">but he, being lofty-minded<br>or &lsquo;proud&rsquo;...<\/span>"},{"st":"11","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ΔΕΝΥΣΟς, Deunysus: — Dionysus. Compare Anacreon:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">and oft loud-shouting Deunysus<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">The ι becoming ε gives Deonysus, which is the Samian form — and by contraction Deunysus, like Theodotus Theudotus.<\/span>"},{"st":"12","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Euripides [&lsquo;the sister of Hector and of many another!]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">τήν κάςιη &lsquo;the sister&rsquo; [not &lsquo;brother&rsquo;]; compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">nor yet the tender sister<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">It is marked because he uses κάσις as a feminine noun, unless indeed it is a shortened form of κασιyvητη &lsquo;sister.&rsquo;<\/span>"},{"st":"13","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Old Etymologicum Magnum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">μύθεαι &lsquo;thou sayest&rsquo;; second person of the present passive; compare Anacreon&rsquo;s έπίδίνεαι:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">thou art crazy for Leucippe.<\/span>"},{"st":"14","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Magnum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ΣΊΛΛΟΙ:—&rsquo; jests,&rsquo; by change of τ to σ, thus some writers for τίλλοι; and τίλλεν means &lsquo;to jest or flout&rsquo;; compare Anacreon: &lsquo;Flouts&rsquo; etc.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Old Etymologicum Magnum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">σίλλος &lsquo;jest&rsquo;... Compare Anacreon in the first Book:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">Lo! this man flouts the blue-bucklered warriors of Ιalysus.<\/span>"},{"st":"15","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on a poem of Hermesianax]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Now Hermesianax is mistaken here in synchronising Sappho and Anacreon, the one having flourished in the reign of Alyattes father of Croesus, and the other in the time of Cyrus and Polycrates. Chamaeleon in his book On Sappho declares that she is held by some authorities to have been the person to whom Anacreon addressed the following poem:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Lo now! golden-haired Love hits me with his purple ball and calls me forth to play with a motley-slippered maid; but no, she hails from grand Lesbos, and so she finds fault with my hair because it is white, and goes gaping after another; and says that Sappho replied to him thus: &lsquo;The hymn thou hast uttered O golden-throned Muse, is that which the illustrious old Teian sang so delightfully from that noble land of fair women&rsquo;; but it is perfectly obvious, surely, that this poem is not the work of Sappho, and for my part I think that Hermesianax is not speaking seriously.<\/span>"},{"st":"16","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Apollonius Syntax<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">THE form χρήμι was actually used for χρω, like φημί, and the third person of it was χρησί, like φησί, with the shortened form χρη &lsquo;it is right or necessary&rsquo;; compare [the form φη &lsquo;he says&rsquo;] in Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">For Targelius saith thou pitchest the quoit full well.<\/span>"},{"st":"17","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Chrysippus Negatives<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ANACREON has said:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">Nor am I easy-going, nor yet pleasant to my fellow-citizens.<\/span>"},{"st":"18","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion Handbook of Metre [on the antispastic]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The catalectic tetrameter which has the second dipody iambic is called Priapeian, for instance:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">I have dined on a morsel of thin mealcake, but I drained a whole keg of wine, and now I thrum delicately my lovely lute in a serenade to dear Poliagre.<\/span>"},{"st":"19","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on the magadis or harp]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">For the sweet Anacreon says:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">And I thrum and thrum in the Lydian fashion the harp of twenty strings, while you, Leucaspis, play the roysterer.<\/span>"},{"st":"20","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Odyssey [&lsquo;the Sintians of wild speech&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Anacreon too speaks of them as makers of weapons:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">What care I for the Scythians and the crook-bowed Sintians who befriend the Cimmerians?<\/span>"},{"st":"21","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion on Poems<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">There are also in poems the so-called επωδοι — the noun is masculine — or epodes, when an addition is made to a long line... but when the addition comes first it is called a pro-ode, as in Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Lo! I climb up and dive from the White Cliff into the hoary wave, drunken with love.<\/span>"},{"st":"22","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on flutes]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">We know also the flutes called half-bores [that is, with half the usual number of holes or stops], of which Anacreon says:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Who turneth back his mind to delightsome youth and danceth to the tender half-bore?<br>These flutes are smaller than the complete ones.<\/span>"},{"st":"23","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion Handbook of Metre [on the Cratinean]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Eupolis in the Exempt from Service has used this form of verse very irregularly; sometimes he writes it thus... and sometimes like this: &lsquo;And I always consorted with good sea-breams,&rsquo; so that it becomes a mixed choriambic like this of Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">I saw Simalus in the chorus with his pretty lyre.<br>Sometimes again he has used it in other irregular shapes.<\/span>"},{"st":"24","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [on the choriambic]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A frequent variety of the choriambic tetrameter catalectic is that which has the second dipody iambic as well as the close; compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-q\">I return from the river bringing all bright...<\/span><span class=\"sm-note\">[the next line perh. began eιματα &lsquo;clothes&rsquo;; the speaker is feminine]<\/span>"},{"st":"25","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [Just before]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Anacreon throughout a whole poem has made the first dipody (of the choriambic tetrameter catalectic) of a tribrach and an iambus, so that there is &lsquo;resolution&rsquo; common both to the choriambic and to the iambic: &lsquo;Light-winged,&rsquo; etc.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Lucian The Gallic Hercules<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">But when I remember that aged Heracles I begin to feel reckless and lose all shame to be doing such things at the statue&rsquo;s time of life; so strength and swiftness and beauty and all other bodily advantages may go hang, and your Love-God, O poet of Teos, may &lsquo;fly by me,&rsquo; etc.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Light-winged I fly to Olympus to fetch master Love; for lo! he will not play with me as he used to do, but he has seen that my beard is getting grey now, and so he flies by me in the wind of his golden-shining wings.<\/span>"},{"st":"26","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on τήγανον &lsquo;pot&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The Ionians say ήγανον without the τ; compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">to throw green-wheat into the pot<\/span>"},{"st":"27","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Priscian Elements of Grammar<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And it is not surprising, since the Greek poets are found to lengthen vocatives in the above termination. Compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-q\">Fair-shining Sun,<\/span><span class=\"sm-note\">[if this poem referred to an eclipse it must have been either 19 May 557 or 17 Feb. 478]<\/span>"},{"st":"28","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Attilius Fortunatianus the Metres of Horace [contrasted with Te deos oro Sybarin cur properas amando<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The second line of the stanza Anacreon gives thus:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">cast his shield into the outflow of a fair-streaming river.<\/span>"},{"st":"29","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Old Etymologicum Magnum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Cuckoo: — a spring bird the size of a falcon; a great coward; compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">as for me, I fled her like a cuckoo.<\/span>"},{"st":"30","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion Handbook of Metre [on &lsquo;unconnectable&rsquo; metres]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Anacreon has added the ithyphallic not to an iambic dipody but to an iambic-mixed choriambic:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">I asked the lyre-maker Strattis if he would wear his hair long.<\/span>"},{"st":"31","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on thirst used metaphorically]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">There is no desire more imperious than that of thirst. And that is why Homer calls Argos &lsquo;much-thirsted-after&rsquo; as being much desired owing to lapse of time [to the absent Greeks], And so too Sophocles says... and Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">You are dear, my lass, to strangers; so, as for me, you may let me go thirsty. [or, keeping the ms-reading, &lsquo;let a thirsty man drink&rsquo;]<\/span>"},{"st":"32","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion Handbook of Metre [on the choriambic catalectic]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">some of these lines end with an amphibrach or bacchius, for instance in the dimeter... and in the trimeter compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">and fell in love with the tearful strife of war.<\/span>"},{"st":"33","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on the large cup or jar called κελεβη]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">compare Anacreon:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">And the serving-maid, holding the jar aloft, poured out the honey-sweet, mixed one in three.<\/span><span class=\"sm-note\">[i. e. one of wine to three of water]<\/span>"},{"st":"34","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Scholiast on Pindar [&lsquo;For in those days the Muse was not covetous nor an hireling, nor were sweet tender-voiced lays sold of honey lipped Terpsichore with their faces silvered o&rsquo;er&rsquo;]: he speaks periphrastically of those who wrote for money. The same sort of thing is said by Anacreon, and possibly there is a reference to it here. Anacreon says:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">nor in those days did Persuasion shine all silver.<\/span>"},{"st":"35","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Attilius Fortunatianus the Metres of Horace<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">In Anacreon we find:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">I will take it to the temple of Hera.<\/span><span class=\"sm-note\">[prob. the famous temple on Cape Colonna in Samos, cf. 21]<\/span>"},{"st":"36","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Iliad [&lsquo;mules... which the Mysians gave once to Priam&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The Mysians, because they dwelt near the Enetians who first bred mules, or because the Mysian mules are particularly good; compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">The Mysians invented the mixing of mare-leaping asses with horses; whence the mules come by the name &lsquo;half-asses.&rsquo;<\/span>"},{"st":"37","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Odyssey [&lsquo;a stormy wind&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The correct form is without ν ζαη... it is Aeolic with the ν and should be accented on the last but one, as in: ⟨... [citation apparently lost; cf. Cram. A.P. 3. 480. 31] in Alcaeus; the form without the ν is Ionic; compare: ⟩<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-q\">... I should live to see my country in misery;<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Anacreon. But Aristarchus says it should be circumflexed (i. e. in the Homeric passage).<\/span>"},{"st":"38","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hesychius Glossary ΕΡΜΑ<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">a support, or a deed, or a rocky place of the sea hidden from view by the waves; compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">I am carried over hidden reefs.<\/span>"},{"st":"39","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Aeschylus [- -. - -.. -. - . - -]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The rhythm is Anacreon&rsquo;s, a broken rhythm suitable to a lament. For Anacreon lived some time at Athens at the time of his passion for Critias, and took delight in the lyrics of Aeschylus. They did not use them promiscuously but only in laments, as Sophocles did... This passage resembles (in rhythm):<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">And will you not suffer me to go home drunk?<\/span>"},{"st":"40","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on garlands]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">They called the garlands they tied about their necks υποθυμίδες; compare... and Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">and woven necklets of lotus did they put about their breasts.<\/span>"},{"st":"41","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Pollux Vocabulary<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">WHEN Anacreon speaks of:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">a wattle basket full of the stalks of fine white celery<br>he means one plaited of reeds.<\/span>"},{"st":"42","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on dancing]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Even Socrates the Wise loved the Memphis dance, and according to Xenophon, when he was found dancing it, as often happened, he used to say to his acquaintance &lsquo;Dancing exercises every limb.&rsquo; For the verb &lsquo;to dance&rsquo; was used of movement and excitement; compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-q\">Lightly danced the fair-tressed daughters of Zeus;<\/span><span class=\"sm-note\">[context seems to imply that the dancing is metaphorical, but?]<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">and Ion: &lsquo;The unexpected makes hearts dance the more.&rsquo;<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"A and B"},{"st":"43","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion Handbook of Metre [on the dactylic]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">... and the tetrameter catalectic in a disyllable, which was first used by Archilochus in epodes... but this metre was afterwards employed by Anacreon for whole poems; compare:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">Sweet-tuned swallow, pretty bird,<\/i> and:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">Lo! baldhead Alexis goes a-wooing.<\/span>"},{"st":"44","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">FOR those which are catalectic in a syllable, compare the two-and-a-half-foot used by Archilochus... and the three-and-a-half-foot used by Anacreon thus:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">this, like all the people<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"BOOK II"},{"st":"45","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-note\">[CF. ATH. 15. 674a, Poll. 6. 107]<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on garlands]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And why in Anacreon are people crowned with osier? In the second Book of his Lyric Poems we read:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">For ten months now has Megistes crowned himself, dear heart, with osier and drunk the honey-sweet must. [prob. means he is an ephebus of 10 months&rsquo; standing]<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">A garland of osier is absurd; for it is used for cords and wickerwork.<\/span>"},{"st":"46","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [on mixing wine]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Anacreon bids them mix the wine still stronger [than one of wine&nbsp; to two of water; Alc. 163] in this passage:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">And into a pure clean jar let them pour five and three.<\/span>"},{"st":"47","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Iliad [&lsquo;in anger over the dice&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Most of the &lsquo;individual&rsquo; editions read &lsquo;in a quarrel over the dice,&rsquo; using the feminine form of the word &lsquo;dice&rsquo;; and it is more Ionic; compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">The dice of Love are madnesses and mellays.<\/span>"},{"st":"48,49","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion Handbook of Metre [on the lonicum a minore]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And the brachycatalectic tetrameter is used for whole poems by Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Lo now! Love like a smith has smitten me with a great hammer and soused me in the chill stream.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on the luxury of Polycrates, tyrant of Samos]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">... Indeed he was actually a rival in love to the poet Anacreon, and in a fit of rage cut his beloved&rsquo;s hair off.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Aelian Historical Miscellanies. Anacreon did not take upon himself to accuse Polycrates with coolness and determination, but shifted the blame to the beloved, in words in which he upbraided his rashness and ignorance in taking arms against his own hair. But the poem on the disaster to the hair must be sung by Anacreon; for he will sing it himself better than I.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Favorinus in Stobaeus Anthology [against beauty]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And therefore Anacreon would seem to be ridiculous and captious in blaming the lad for having cut off some of his hair, in the words:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">You have shorn a faultless flower of soft hair,<br>[arming your own hand against your tresses].<\/span>"},{"st":"50","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Magnum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ΣΊΤΟΙ &lsquo;corn&rsquo;:... The word σείω &lsquo;to shake&rsquo; occurs also in the form σίω, which is used by Anacreon, for instance:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">tossing [your] Thracian locks<\/span>"},{"st":"51","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion Handbook of Metre [on the Ionicum a minore]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Of the trimeter the acatalectic... and in Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">May my due be granted me, to die; for no other deliverance from these troubles e&rsquo;er can be.<\/span>"},{"st":"52","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Pindar [&lsquo;a golden-horned hind&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The poets make a point of giving the female deer horns... and it is the rule in Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">mild-eyed, like a little suckling fawn that is afraid when he is left by his horned mother in the wood.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Zenodotus changed κεροεσσης &lsquo;horned&rsquo; to εροεσσης &lsquo;lovely&rsquo; because it is recorded that the females have no horns like the males; nevertheless all the poets give them horns.<\/span>"},{"st":"53","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">ETYMOLOGICUM MAGNUM:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Doorkeepers that fight are a mischief, from the second Book of Anacreon; σινάμωροι &lsquo;a mischief,&rsquo; that is μεμορημενοι &lsquo;destined to mischief,&rsquo; σίνεσθαι.<\/span>"},{"st":"54","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Apollonius Homeric Lexicon<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">θεσθαι &lsquo;to deposit&rsquo;:... for θησαυρός &lsquo;treasure&rsquo; is sometimes called θεσμος; compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">and carried off a great treasure.<\/span>"},{"st":"55","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on the game called cottabus]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">They regularly practised the cottabus, — a Sicilian game as Anacreon of Teos testifies:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">throwing with elbow curved the drops of the Sicilian cottabus.<\/span>"},{"st":"56","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [on garlands]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">They also wore garlands on their foreheads, as the beautiful Anacreon says:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Let us put little garlands of celery upon our brows and hold high festival to Dionysus.<\/span>"},{"st":"57","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion Handbook of Metre [on the Ionicum a minore]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And in Anacreon (51)... hut the catalectic trimeter is different: [the second foot being a molossus]<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">the prancing Bassarids of Dionysus [i. e. Bacchants prancing: or of straddling gait]<\/span>"},{"st":"58","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Euripides [&lsquo;leaving her dear bed in a single garment like a Dorian maid&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">... and &lsquo;to play the Dorian&rsquo; means that women display themselves naked; compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">to put off her shift and play the Dorian<\/span>"},{"st":"59","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Ptolemaeus differences in words: [διαβόητος &lsquo;famous&rsquo;]: επιβόητος means &lsquo;of ill report&rsquo;; compare Anacreon&rsquo;s second Book:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">and you will make me of ill-report among my neighbours.<\/span>"},{"st":"60","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Odyssey [a vexed passage]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">μύθου &lsquo;word, tale&rsquo;: — here &lsquo;rebellion&rsquo; or &lsquo;factious strife&rsquo;; hence Anacreon calls the rebel fishermen of Samos μυθηταί; compare:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">And rebels, O Megistes, bear sway in the sacred city of the Nymphs.<\/span>"},{"st":"61","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Ptolemaeus differences in Words: λεια with an ε means &lsquo;cattle-lifting&rsquo;; compare Homer Iliad 677 &lsquo;A mightily abundant prey did we drive together out of the plain&rsquo;; whereas with the ι it is an adverb expressing intensity, whether the ι is short as in Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">thou art exceedingly afraid<\/span>"},{"st":"62","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Amatorius<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">SO true is it that the genuine love is of the male, not &lsquo;shining with desire,&rsquo; as Anacreon says of that of the female, nor &lsquo;gleaming with unguents,&rsquo; but of plain aspect and not spoilt in the schools of the philosophers.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">e.g.... shining with desire and gleaming with unguents<\/span>"},{"st":"63","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion Handbook of Metre [on &lsquo;unconnectable&rsquo; metres]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A notable composite too is the double two-and-a-half-foot line known as the encomiologic, which consists of a two-and-a-half-foot dactylic and an iambic of the same length, used by Alcaeus... and by Anacreon in several poems, for instance<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Warman Ares loves a staunch fighter.<\/span>"},{"st":"64","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Elymologicum Magnum: ΉΜΕΤΕΡΕΙΟΣ is a possessive adjective meaning &lsquo;belonging to what is ours&rsquo;; it was used by Anacreon thus:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">neither one of our land nor beautiful<\/span>"},{"st":"65","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Pindar [&lsquo;when they set about to make a crown for Ilium&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">&lsquo;crown&rsquo; is metaphorical for &lsquo;wall&rsquo;; for the walls of a city are as it were its crown; so also Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">But alas! the crown of the city is destroyed.<\/span>"},{"st":"66","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Choeroboscus on Hephaestion [on the combination of two syllables not separated by a consonant]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Heliodorus in his Introduction says that even three syllables coalesce into one, for instance this double two-and-a-half line which resembles an elegiac:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Asteris, neither I love you nor Apelles.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">For it is not an elegiac really, but the first part is a dactylic and the second an iambic, since it has two iambic feet and a syllable, so that the words φιλεω οΰ together make a short and one long.<\/span>"},{"st":"67","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Magnum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ΗΠΕΡΟΠΕΥΤΗς &lsquo;a cheat&rsquo;:... compare (Od. 11. 364)... and Anacreon:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">... wishes to be a deceiver to us.<\/span>"},{"st":"68","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ΆΒΑΚΉς &lsquo;childlike, innocent&rsquo;... so from αβακης comes ίβακω &lsquo;to be childlike,&rsquo; as εύσεβω &lsquo;to be pious&rsquo; from ευσεβης&rsquo;pious&rsquo;; there is also a form άβακίζω; compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">But as for me I hate all those who have secretive and uncompromising ways; I have learnt that you, Megistes, are one of the childlike ones; meaning quiet and not blustering.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-note\">[more prob. &lsquo;frank&rsquo;]<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"BOOK III"},{"st":"69","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Stobaeus Anthology [on death and its inevitability]; Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">My temples have grown grey and my crown bare and white; graceful youth is no longer with me, and my teeth are the teeth of an old man. There is left me but a short span of sweet life. And so I often make my moan for fear of the underworld. For dire is the dark hold of death, and grievous the way down thither; and more, &rsquo;tis sure that once down there&rsquo;s no coming up.<\/span>"},{"st":"70,71,72","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Maximus of tyre Dissertations: The art of the sophist of Teos is of the same kind and character. He is in love with all who are beautiful and praises them all. His poems are full of the hair of Smerdis, the eyes of Cleobulus, and the youthful bloom of Bathyllus. Yet mark even in this his powers of restraint:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">and I long to play with you; you have such pretty ways;<\/i> and again:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">To be just and fair is a good thing in lovers; and I am sure he has revealed his art at once in the lines:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">For as for me, the children can but love me for my words and my tunes, seeing that I sing pretty things and know how to say pretty things.<\/span>"},{"st":"73","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Strabo Geography [on the Carians]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Their bent for things military is indicated, it is said, by our shield-straps, blazons, and plumes, all of which are called Carian. Compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Lo! through the shield-strap of Carian work have I thrust my hand.<\/span>"},{"st":"74","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion Handbook of Metre [on the lonicum a minore]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The acatalectic dimeter of &lsquo;irregular&rsquo; type is frequent in Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Lo now! I went down to Pythomander&rsquo;s to escape Love.<\/span>"},{"st":"75","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on mixing wine]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">It was formerly the custom to pour into the cup first the water and then the wine... Compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Bring water, lad, bring wine, bring me garlands of flowers; aye, bring them hither; for I would try a bout with Love.<\/span>"},{"st":"76","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [on the same]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">In Anacreon it is one of wine to two of water:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Come bring me a jar, lad; I want my first-drink; ten ladles of water to five of wine, for I would e&rsquo;en play the Bacchanal in decent wise; and proceeding he calls the drinking of wine unmixed a Scythian practice:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Come let us give up this Scythian drinking with uproar and din over our cups, and drink moderately between pretty songs of praise. [the two passages are apparently continuous]<\/span>"},{"st":"77","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Hesiod [&lsquo;of the infernal God&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The word χθόνιος &lsquo;infernal&rsquo; is used for στυγερος &lsquo;hateful or wretched&rsquo; as in Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-q\">and I kept myself secretive.<\/span><span class=\"sm-note\">[if the quotation is sounds it is hardly applicable]<\/span>"},{"st":"78","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Pindar [&lsquo;Even as if one... shall make gift of a cup to his daughter&rsquo;s young bridegroom&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">πρόπινειν, &lsquo;to drink before or to, to pledge,&rsquo; is properly to make a present of the drinking-vessel along with its contents; compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Nay, pledge me, friend, thy slender thighs; where pledge means &lsquo;let me have.&rsquo;<\/span>"},{"st":"79","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Magnum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ΠΗΓΟς &lsquo;the purple coverlet&rsquo;; for ρεξαι is equivalent to βαψαι &lsquo;to dye&rsquo;... the use of ραγευς for βαφευς &lsquo;dyer&rsquo; and ρεγος for βάμμα &lsquo;dye&rsquo; is shown by Anacreon&rsquo;s phrase:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">dye of sea-purple<\/span>"},{"st":"80","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Proclus on Hesiod [&lsquo;a woman... with cozening babble&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">κωτίλλουσα means &lsquo;sweetly speaking&rsquo;; he uses the verb in connexion with the swallow, as Anacreon does:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">the babbling swallow<\/span>"},{"st":"81","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Servius on the Aeneid [&lsquo;Dido... and drank long love&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">metaphor from a drinking-party; so Anacreon<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">drinking love<\/span>"},{"st":"82","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">&lsquo;mad Love&rsquo;: — by &lsquo;metonymy&rsquo; Love that makes mad... compare Anacreon<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">languishing love<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"A"},{"st":"82","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Herodian Words Without Parallel<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">But Anacreon gives the word χαριεις<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">graceful<br>the full form χαριτοεις.<\/span>"},{"st":"83","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">PHOTIUS LEXICON:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">the talents of Tantalus<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">The Phrygian Tantalus was famous for his wealth, being reputed the son of Zeus and Pluto; the proverb is used by Anacreon in his third Book.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"BOOK IV. IAMBICS"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-note\">[I have included trochaics]<\/span>"},{"st":"84","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Heracleitus Homeric allegories: Moreover Anacreon of Teos, in finding fault with the airs of a courtesan and the arrogance of a disdainful woman, has compared her skittish disposition to a horse in these lines:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Pray, why do you look askance at me, my Thracian filly, and shun me so resolutely as though I knew nothing of my art? I would have you to know I could bridle you right well and take rein and ride you about the turning-post of the course. But instead you graze in the meadows and frisk and frolic to your heart&rsquo;s content; for you have not a clever breaker to ride you.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-note\">[perh, emending text, &lsquo;for it is because I am so clever a breaker that I keep you unridden.&rsquo;]<\/span>"},{"st":"85","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion Handbook of Metre [on the trochaic]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Among the acatalectic forms the tetrameter is a notable metre, for instance Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Give ear, thou maid of the lovely tresses and robe of gold, give ear to an old man&rsquo;s prayer. [prob. to a Muse]<\/span>"},{"st":"86","sp":"Julian The Beard-Hater","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">For already, as you see as well as I do, I am near, save the mark, to the time<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"Julian The Beard-Hater","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">When white hairs shall mingle with my black in the words of the poet of Teos.<\/span>"},{"st":"87","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A tetrameter by Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Of all my gallant friends, Aristocleides, I pity thee the most; for in the defence of thy country from slavery thou hast lost thy youth.<\/span>"},{"st":"88","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">PLATO THEAGES:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"Socr","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Well, shall we make use now of Euripides, Theages? It is he, I think, Who says &lsquo;Kings know their art through converse with the knowing.&rsquo; Now suppose someone said to Euripides, knowing in what?... Well then, shall I tell you the answer?<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"Theag","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Please do.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"Socr","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">They are knowing in what Anacreon says Callicrite knew. You know the poem, don&rsquo;t you?<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">THEAG, Yes.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"Socr","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Well? Is the converse you want the converse or company of a man who is of the same craft as Callicrite daughter of Cyane, and knows how to king it or queen it as the poet says that she did, so that you yourself may become a ruler over us and our country?<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"Socr","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">e.g. Callicrite daughter of Cyane knows well how to queen it.<\/span>"},{"st":"89","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Sophocles [&lsquo;swaying he fell on the unyielding earth&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">τανταλωθείς means &lsquo;shaken&rsquo; or &lsquo;swaying,&rsquo; witness Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-q\">swings amid the dark-leaved laurel and green olive<\/span><span class=\"sm-note\">[perh. of a bird]<\/span>"},{"st":"90","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">An Anonymous Writer On Solecism<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The ancients called barbarous speakers solecians; compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Make cease, O Zeus, the solecian utterance.<\/span>"},{"st":"91,92","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Iliad [&lsquo;a lion that has eaten up a bull&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">the severance of the verb &lsquo;eaten up&rsquo; from its prefix is descriptive of the disparted bull, as it is not necessary to the metre; compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">cut through the midst of the neck<\/i> and:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">the robe was rent right down.<\/span>"},{"st":"93","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Cramer Inedita (Oxford)<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">μεμετρεαται &lsquo;have been measured&rsquo;: — this form is Ionic; compare Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">and my senses are stunned.<\/span>"},{"st":"94","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on σκύφος &lsquo;a drinking-vessel&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Hesiod in the second Book of the Melampodia uses the form with π inserted... so also Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">but as for me, I held the cup and drained it to the white-crested Erxion;<br>&lsquo;drained it to him&rsquo; instead of &lsquo;drank it to him.&rsquo;<\/span>"},{"st":"95","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-note\">[Cf. Poll. 6. 107 who explains &lsquo;Naucr.&rsquo; as &lsquo;of marjoram&rsquo;]<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Now that our enquiries have turned to garlands, pray tell us, Ulpian, the nature of the &lsquo;wreath of Naucratis&rsquo; mentioned by the charming Anacreon; for that delicious poet says:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">and each man had three garlands, of roses two, and the other a wreath of Naucratis.<\/span>"},{"st":"96,97","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on luxury]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Chamaeleon of Pontus in his book On Anacreon, after quoting the lines:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-q\">the flaxen-haired Eurypyle&rsquo;s concern is with Litter-rider Artemon;<\/span><span class=\"sm-note\">[not part of the next poem: for Euryp. cf. A. P. 7. 27]<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">declares that Artemon received this nickname from his luxurious habit of being carried in a litter. And indeed Anacreon avers in the following passage that he rose from poverty to luxury:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Once he went about in the wasped-headdress of a Cimmerian, with wooden astragals in his ears, and about his ribs a hairy oxhide that had been the unwashen cover of a wretched shield — the scoundrel Artemon who made a fraudulent living by consorting with bread-wenches and whores-for-choice, with his neck often bound to the whipping-stock [according to Poll. 10. 177 fraudulent buyers or sellers in the market were thus treated] or else to the wheel, and his back often seared with the leathern scourge and his hair and beard plucked out; but now he goes in a coach, wearing earrings of gold like a mix-with-all, and carries an ivory sunshade as though he were a woman.<\/span>"},{"st":"98","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion Handbook of Metre [on the iambic]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">There are notable acatalectic forms of it, whether dimeters such as whole poems of Anacreon (104)... or trimeters such as: &lsquo;Ye are&rsquo; etc.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Plutarch Against the Stoics<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">So when they are thirsty they have no need of water, nor when hungry of bread:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Ye are like kind guests who need but roof and fire.<\/span>"},{"st":"99","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Zenobius Proverbs<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">IT is said that the Carians when at war with Darius the Persian, in obedience to an old oracle bidding them take the bravest of men for their allies, went to Branchidae and asked the God there if they should seek alliance with Miletus; whereupon he replied:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">There was a time when the Milesians were brave men:<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">but the line occurs earlier in Anacreon.<\/span>"},{"st":"100","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">SELECT PROVERBS:<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Thanks to Syloson there&rsquo;s plenty of room:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Syloson of Samos became friendly with the Persian king Darius, and through his means succeeded to the despotism at Samos on the death of Polycrates, but owing to the harshness of his rule most of the inhabitants emigrated. Hence the proverb.<\/span>"},{"st":"101","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Herodian on Incorrect Speech<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">There is a difference between -γημαι &lsquo;to marry&rsquo; and γημασθαι &lsquo;to give oneself in marriage, be married&rsquo;... Anacreon, ridiculing a man for effeminacy, says:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">and the chamber wherein he married not but was married.<\/span>"},{"st":"102","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Magnum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ΚΝΰΖΑ, according to Herodian, if it means the plant &lsquo;Fleabane,&rsquo; is a shortening... but if it means &lsquo;spoilt or denied&rsquo; it comes from κνύω &lsquo;to scratch&rsquo; — whence κνυος &lsquo;the itch&rsquo; — ; compare Anacreon in the Iambics:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">I am growing blear-eyed and big because of thy lechery. [the subject is fem: the meaning &lsquo;blear-eyed&rsquo; or &lsquo;with itching eyes&rsquo; is more likely than &lsquo;defiled&rsquo;]<\/span>"},{"st":"103","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Zonaras [on ΠΑΝΔΟΚΕΙΟΝ &lsquo;inn&rsquo;]... the form μοχλός &lsquo;bolt&rsquo; with a χ is used equally in Attic, Doric, and Ionic, save for Anacreon, who is practically alone in spelling it with a κ, though Zenodotus would change it to a χ:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-q\">He sleeps untroubled though he never drew bolt in his house-door.<\/span><span class=\"sm-note\">[lit. double-door]<\/span>"},{"st":"104","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion Handbook of Metre [on the iambic]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">There are notable acatalectic forms of it, whether dimeters such as whole poems of Anacreon, for instance:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Lo! I both love and love not, and am mad yet not mad.<\/span>"},{"st":"105","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on πίνω &lsquo;to drink]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Then, my friend, to quote the Twins of Alexis &lsquo;Drink you to this man and he to another&rsquo; and let us have what the lyric poet Anacreon calls a hearth-cup:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">and babble not like a wave of the sea, while you quaff the bounteous hearth-cup with the jade Gastrodora;<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">[prob. a comic perversion of Metrodora (Wil.)]this is what we call the equalisation-cup.<\/span>"},{"st":"106","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion Handbook of Metre [on the iambic]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The catalectic dimeter is that known as Anacreontean, for instance:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">He that will fight may fight if he will.<\/span>"},{"st":"107","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Priscian the Metres of Terence<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">According to Heliodorus, Anacreon has the line:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">O ever too delightful one! for many are they that love thee;<br>where the iambic line has a spondee in the fourth place.<\/span>"},{"st":"108","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Magnum ΚΙΝΆΚΗ &lsquo;scimitar&rsquo;<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">the form is κινάκης in Sophocles. The form used by Anacreon (is not parallel, for it) is written without the ι (&lsquo;subscript&rsquo;); there has been ecthlipsis or squeezing-out of the ι, and with crasis of ωα the result is τωκινάκη<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">with his scimitar<br>as in ωπολλον for ω Απολλον &lsquo;O Apollo.&rsquo;<\/span>"},{"st":"109","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Etymologicum sorbonicum ΑΣΤΡΑΒΗ... in Anacreon it occurs in the form αστραφη<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">saddle<br>and is used of a chair-saddle in which people sit securely.<\/span>"},{"st":"110","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hesychius Glossary ΑΎΤΆΓΗΤΟΙ<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">&lsquo;self-admiring, self-marvelling&rsquo; (women); Ion in the Alcmena. Some authorities say it means<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">stubborn<\/i> and it is used so by Anacreon.<\/span>"},{"st":"111","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Magnum ΙΣΥΝΗΚΐΝ<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Alcaeus uses this form, and Anacreon εξυνηκεν<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">he understood,<br>with pleonastic augment.<\/span>"},{"st":"112","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Aristophanes<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Because of its warming the breast the phrase &lsquo;to put the corslet on&rsquo; is used meaning &lsquo;to be drunk,&rsquo; and people slightly drunk were called<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">top-corsleted<\/i> an expression used by Anacreon. It is Attic.<\/span>"},{"st":"113","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Gudianum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ΚΟΡΩΝΗ &lsquo;crow&rsquo;... from καυρος which means &lsquo;bad&rsquo;: compare Anacreon:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">stepping with arched neck<br>[i.e. haughtily].<\/span>"},{"st":"114","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica [&lsquo;glancing fearfully over them&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">looking at them hard and excitedly; for παμφαλαv<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">to glance fearfully<\/i> is to look at excitedly, and is used by Hipponax and Anacreon.<\/span>"},{"st":"115","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Clement of Alexandria The Schoolmaster<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">For effeminacy of movement in walking about and <i class=\"sm-qi\">to go straddling-wise<\/i><\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">in Anacreon&rsquo;s phrase, are altogether marks of the harlot. [1 cf. Semon. 18 (Bergk), to whom the fr. possibly belongs]<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"HDR","t":"BOOK V. ELEGIACS"},{"st":"116","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on drinking-parties]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And the delightful Anacreon says:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">I like not him who at his drinking beside the full mixing-bowl tells of strife and lamentable war, but rather one that taketh thought for delightsome mirth by mingling the Muses and the splendid gifts of Aphrodite.<\/span>"},{"st":"117","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hephaestion Handbook of Metre [on &lsquo;common&rsquo; syllables]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">It is also found in epic metres, for instance in Theocritus... and in Anacreon&rsquo;s Elegiacs:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Neither is it a thing to your mind, but nevertheless I await you without doubting.<\/span>"},{"st":"118","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">[Longinus] on the Sublime<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">... Most productive and fruitful [of such an effect?] is this of Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">I care no more for the Thracian ⟨[filly.⟩ [emendation uncertain]<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">In this respect too this phrase of Theopompus is worthy of praise — it seems to me the analogy makes it most impressive — : &lsquo;Philip being adept at eating circumstances as a duty.&rsquo; You see that the homely phrase is sometimes far more vivid than the embellishment; for it is immediately recognised from everyday life, and we are quicker to believe a thing when it is familiar to us.<\/span>"},{"st":"119","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on cups]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And Anacreon says:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">and I am become a wine-bibber.<\/span>"},{"st":"120","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Macedonius:... nor would I like rigid Aristotle sit in judgement on myself; for &rsquo;tis the counsel of Anacreon that I keep in mind —<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">let not Care restrain.<\/span>"},{"st":"121","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Hyginus astronomies: for this reason it is said by some authorities that the Lyre, which comes next to that constellation, is the lyre of Theseus, for among his manifold accomplishments he seems to have been skilled in the lyre. And Anacreon says the same:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-q\">The lyre is near to Aegid Theseus.<\/span><span class=\"sm-note\">[Mein, perh. rightly ascribes this line to a later Anacreon who wrote Astronomica]<\/span>"},{"st":"122","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Eustathius on the Iliad<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Moreover Anacreon calls such a woman quite coolly &lsquo;all-given&rsquo; and &lsquo;people-trodden,&rsquo; and &lsquo;sung-of-many.&rsquo;<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Suidas Lexicon μυσαχνη<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">— &lsquo;harlot&rsquo; in Archilochus...Anacreon calls her &lsquo;all-given&rsquo; and &lsquo;people-trodden,&rsquo; and (mad-tail?)e.g. All-given, sung - of - many, people-trodden, apple-orchard<\/span>"},{"st":"123","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">HESYCHIUS GLOSSARY:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">Son of Aethopia<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">that is &lsquo;Dionysus&rsquo;; Anacreon. Some authorities say that Aethopia means &lsquo;wine,&rsquo; others &lsquo;Artemis.&rsquo;<\/span>"},{"st":"124","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Scholiast on the Iliad [&lsquo;the arms the God hath given are such as the work of immortals should be and as no mortal man should have made&rsquo;]: Since the fact is of its nature an hyperbole it admits of no extension; compare Anacreon;<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">he revels as Dionysus revels<\/i> where Dionysus is compared with himself.<\/span>"},{"st":"125","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on meals]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Telemachus&rsquo; tables remained before the guests full during the whole of the entertainment as is still the custom among many Barbarian nations,<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">overspread with all manner of good things<\/i> as Anacreon says.<\/span>"},{"st":"126","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Gregorius on Hermogenes<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The ear is improperly flattered by this figure when it is erotic in character, I mean as it is used by Sappho and Anacreon; for instance &lsquo;as white as milk,&rsquo; &lsquo;as soft as water,&rsquo; &lsquo;as tuneful as the lyre,&rsquo; &lsquo;as skittish as a mare,&rsquo; &lsquo;as delicate as a rose,&rsquo; &lsquo;as soft as a fine robe,&rsquo; &lsquo;as precious as gold.&rsquo;<\/span>"},{"st":"127","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hesychius Glossary<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ΆΜΊΘΑ: — a kind of eatable or sauce mentioned by Anacreon.<\/span>"},{"st":"128","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Eustathius on the Odyssey<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Whence is said to come Anacreon&rsquo;s use of άνηλατος to mean:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">disobedient,<br>from beasts of burden.<\/span>"},{"st":"129","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Athenaeus Doctors at Dinner [on stringed instruments]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The baromus and the barbitus mentioned by Sappho and Anacreon, as well as the magadis and the trigonon and the sambuca, are all ancient.<\/span>"},{"st":"130","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Pollux Vocabulary<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ANACREON uses διτοκος to mean her <i class=\"sm-qi\">that has twice brought forth<\/i><\/span>"},{"st":"131","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">THE participles are τερπων &lsquo;pleasing&rsquo; — and there is the aorist ετερψεν &lsquo;he pleased&rsquo; — ; ηδων &lsquo;pleasing,&rsquo; which is Ionic; and the aorist ησε<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">he pleased<\/i> though rare with us, occurs in Anacreon who was an Ionian and a poet.<\/span>"},{"st":"132","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [on women&rsquo;s head-adornments]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And you might add... the <i class=\"sm-qi\">flower-cups<\/i> used by Homer and Anacreon.<\/span>"},{"st":"133","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ΠΤΎΣΜΑ &lsquo;spittle&rsquo;... the words that come from it are πτυειν &lsquo;to spit,&rsquo; αποπτύειν &lsquo;to spit out,&rsquo; καταπτύειν &lsquo;to spit upon,&rsquo; κατάπτυστος &lsquo;contemptible,&rsquo; άπάπτυστος &lsquo;detestable&rsquo;; Anacreon uses the separate feminine form κατάπτυστη<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">contemptible<\/span>"},{"st":"134","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Aeschylus [&lsquo;soft-living Lydians&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">They live softly, whence Anacreon&rsquo;s phrase:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">Lydian-like persons<br>meaning &lsquo;luxurious.&rsquo;<\/span>"},{"st":"135","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Strabo Geography [on Ionia]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">The first founder of Teos was Athamas, and that is why Anacreon calls it<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Athamantid<\/span>"},{"st":"136","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Pollux Vocabulary<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">FROM οινος &lsquo;wine&rsquo;... οίνοπότης &lsquo;wine-bibber,&rsquo; and οινοποτις <i class=\"sm-qi\">wine-bibbing woman<\/i> in Anacreon.<\/span>"},{"st":"137","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">AND <i class=\"sm-qi\">wine-server<\/i> in Anacreon.<\/span>"},{"st":"138","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Hesychius Glossary<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ΓΥΝΑΙΚΕς ειλιποδες &lsquo;shambling women&rsquo;... and Anacreon says:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">twining thigh with thigh<\/span>"},{"st":"139","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica [&lsquo;Hera clasped her soft hand&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ραδινης &lsquo;soft, tender&rsquo;; but Anacreon uses it to mean &lsquo;swift&rsquo; [the explanation is hardly correct]:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">slender colts<\/span>"},{"st":"140","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Choeroboscus on Theodosius Canons<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">We remark in Homer the phrase &lsquo;the soiled clothes which I have by me&rsquo;... and in Anacreon<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">beflogged back<\/span>"},{"st":"141","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Servius on the Aeneid [&lsquo;and fears for his dear burden&rsquo; Camilla]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Compare Anacreon:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">a burden of love<\/span>"},{"st":"142","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Apollonius of Rhodes Argonautica [&lsquo;through the beautiful sleeping-chamber&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">... &lsquo;beautiful&rsquo; either because the room was royal, or because it was connected with love; for that is how we speak of what belongs to our beloved. So Anacreon of the woman he loved....<\/span>"},{"st":"143","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Pollux Vocabulary<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ACCORDING to Anacreon they used to crown themselves with myrtle, and coriander, and willow; and with the wreath of Naucratis, which was marjoram; and also with anise, which is mentioned both by Sappho and by Alcaeus.<\/span>"},{"st":"144","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Himerius Declamations [to Basilius]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Hail! dear light that smilest with so fair a face; for I will take a tune from the lyre to sing in honour of your visit, and though I would rather prevail upon the actual words to be my lyre and poesy so that I might sing you some wanton thing such as Simonides or Pindar sang to Dionysus and Apollo, I will content myself, since the words are proud and haughty and unmanageable and frisk it outside the limits of metre, with inviting Poesy to give me some tune of Teos, which is a Muse I love, and bring you this song of praise from the stores of Anacreon, adding to it something of my own: O light of Greece and of all us who dwell in the holy plain of Pallas and the groves of the Muses...<\/span>"},{"st":"145","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same [to Cerbonius]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Come then, my children, since this man illumines us like a God such as poets clothe in mortal guise and various shapes and bring into towns and cities &lsquo;to note the lawlessness or orderliness of man,&rsquo; such as Homer makes Athena, and Anacreon and Euripides make Dionysus...<\/span>"},{"st":"146","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Zenobius Proverbs<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">&lsquo;PROUDER than Peleus of his sword&rsquo;: — this proverb is mentioned by Anacreon and by Pindar in his Nemeans (4. 95); it is said that the sword was made by Hephaestus and given to Peleus by the Gods because of his virtuous behaviour, and by using it he always succeeded whether in battle or the chase.<\/span>"},{"st":"147","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Fulgentius mythologies: for according to Anacreon, a most ancient authority, when Zeus took arms against the Titans — that is the sons of Titan brother of Saturn or Cronus — and had made sacrifice to Heaven, he saw an eagle fly in such a way as to make a favourable omen of his victory; and for this happy augury, and the more because it was followed by victory, he made a golden eagle upon his war-standards, and consecrated it as a protection to his valour; whence are derived the Roman standards of the like sort.<\/span>"},{"st":"148","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on Horace [&lsquo;nor has Time destroyed the playful work of old Anacreon&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">Anacreon wrote a satire (on his enemy Lysander?). Some authorities say that he wrote the story of Circe and Penelope &lsquo;loving the same man.&rsquo;<\/span>"},{"st":"149","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Palatine Anthology<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ANACREON of Teos on the soldier Agathon, at Abdera:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">The doughty Agathon who died for Abdera, was mourned at his pyre by all this town; for blood-loving Ares never slew in the whirl of hateful battle such a youth as he.<\/span>"},{"st":"150","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ON Timocritus for distinguished valour; by Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">This is the tomb of Timocritus, a staunch man in the wars; for it is the craven, not the brave, that are spared by Ares.<\/span>"},{"st":"151","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A dedication; by Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">She with the thyrse is Heliconias, she next her Xanthippe, and she that is joining the others Glauce; and they come from the hill with ivy and a fat bunch of grapes and a kid for Dionysus.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-note\">[title of dedicatory picture or plaque]<\/span>"},{"st":"152","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A dedication to Zeus by Pheidolas; by the same:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">This mare of Pheidolas from spacious Corinth is set up as a memorial of the prowess of her legs.<\/span>"},{"st":"153","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A dedication; by the same:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">This robe was made by Praxidice and designed by Dyseris: the art of it is common to them both.<\/span>"},{"st":"154","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A dedication to Apollo by Naucrates; by the same:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Lord of the Silver Bow, hearken to these prayers, and give thy gracious thanks to Naucrates son of Aeschylus.<\/span>"},{"st":"155","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">BY the same:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">I was first set here in effigy by Calliteles; this later image of me is put up by his children, to whom give thou thanks.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-note\">[a herm with what appears to be an incorrect restoration (omitting μ) of this inscr., in letters dating 460-445, has been found at Athens.]<\/span>"},{"st":"156","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A dedication by Praxagoras; by the same:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">These gifts to the Gods are the offering of Praxagoras son of Lycaeus, and the work of Anaxagoras.<\/span>"},{"st":"157","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A dedication by Melanthus to Semele; by the same:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">I was set up to the garland-loving son of Semele by Melanthus son of Areiphilus in memory of the victory of his chorus.<\/span>"},{"st":"158","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A dedication to Athena; by the same:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">I who hang here in the precinct of Athena am the shield which brought Python safe home from ill-sounding war.<\/span>"},{"st":"159","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A dedication to Dionysus; by the same:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">To thy honour, Dionysus, and as a fine adornment of the city was I set up by Echecratidas lord of Thessaly.<\/span>"},{"st":"160","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">A dedication to Hermes by Timonax; by the same:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Pray you that the Herald of the Gods be kind to Timonax, who set me up to the Lord Hermes as an adornment of the pretty porch; I receive into my gymnasium any who will come, friend or stranger.<\/span>"},{"st":"161","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ANACREON:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">In gratitude for these pretty gifts, O Son of Maia, send Tellias a life to be desired, and grant he may dwell among the upright-ruling people of Euonymia [a deme of Attica] enjoying the lot of happy days.<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-note\">[inscr. for a statue of Hermes (set up by a resident alien outside the house, Wil.)]<\/span>"},{"st":"162","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">The Same<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">ON Cleenorides lost at sea; by Anacreon:<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">Thou too, Cleenorides, didst put thy trust in the wintry blast of the Southwind and wast slain by thy love of thy home; for the season with whom is no covenanting had kept thee fast, and now the wet waves have washed away thy pleasant youth.<\/span>"},{"st":"163","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">BEKKER INCDITA: ΑΚΤΑΙΝΩΣΑΙ<br>to uplift<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">meaning to raise, to lift, to elevate; and it is formed thus: — there is a tree called ακτεα or ακτή &lsquo;elder,&rsquo; from which they make darts; so Anacreon.<\/span>"},{"st":"164","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Eustathius on the Iliad [&lsquo;sits on both feet&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">because people who sit thus on both feet can be called sarcastically knock-kneed, as is shown by the ancient writers where they say that crooked-legged people are<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">knock-kneed but Anacreon uses the word of cowards.<\/span>"},{"st":"165","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Apollonius Adverbs [on adverbs in -στί]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">With this in view Tryphon investigated the forms μεγαλωστί &lsquo;greatly&rsquo; and ιρωστί<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">sacredly<\/i> in Anacreon.<\/span>"},{"st":"166","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Eustathius on the Odyssey<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">⟨φω⟩ is equivalent to λεγω &lsquo;to say,&rsquo; and has an aorist participle φασας — as κλω has κλάσας — by shortening, φάς, as... just as Heracleides quotes the form κλάς<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\"><i class=\"sm-qi\">having broken off<\/i> from Anacreon.<\/span>"},{"st":"167","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-note\">[CF. ORION 148. 5]<\/span><span class=\"sm-src\">Etymologicum Magnum<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">And σαλαιζειν is used by Anacreon meaning <i class=\"sm-qi\">to wail<\/i><\/span>"},{"st":"168","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-src\">Scholiast on the Iliad [&lsquo;perish unpraised&rsquo;]<\/span><span class=\"sm-ctx\">according to some authorities, &lsquo;unlamented&rsquo;; Anacreon uses ύμνος<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-frag\">song of praise<br>to mean a dirge.<\/span>"},{"st":"169","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">Herodian Words without Parallel:<\/span><span class=\"sm-q\">Phillus<\/span>"},{"st":"—","sp":"N","t":"<span class=\"sm-ctx\">is a proper name in Anacreon. [the names Phillo and Philleas occur in inscriptions, and Phillus in Herodas 3. 60]<\/span>"}],[]);})();